#!/bin/sh
cat << 'EOF'
Git Server - Available Commands
================================

list
  Lists all Git repositories on the server.
  Returns repository names with .git extension.

  Usage:
    ssh -p 2222 git@host list

create <repository-name>
  Creates a new bare Git repository.
  Repository name must contain only alphanumeric characters, dots, dashes, and underscores.
  Automatically adds .git extension if not present.

  Usage:
    ssh -p 2222 git@host create myproject

  Example:
    ssh -p 2222 git@host create my-app
    # Creates: my-app.git

help
  Displays this help message.

  Usage:
    ssh -p 2222 git@host help

Git Operations
==============

Clone a repository:
  git clone ssh://git@host:2222/home/git/<repository-name>.git

Push to a repository:
  git remote add origin ssh://git@host:2222/home/git/<repository-name>.git
  git push -u origin main

Pull from a repository:
  git pull origin main

Notes
=====

- All repositories are bare repositories (no working directory)
- SSH key authentication is required
- Git-shell restricts access to Git commands only
- Repository names have a maximum length of 100 characters
- Contact your administrator to add/remove SSH keys

EOF
